  "request_sha256": "64f901002a338ffe23b2028552f3da0d02362cc8c318892677b3ef339faa3ebd",
  "response": {
    "data": {
      "A": "Research a low-overhead, single-threaded integer-sorting backend; initially leave generation, validation and hashing unchanged. Compare stable LSD radix designs using four 8-bit passes versus 11/11/10-bit passes. Preserve signed native-int ordering through sign-bit normalization rather than assuming every generated value is positive. Compute global digit histograms together where profitable; omit a pass only when its actual histogram proves it redundant. Investigate small-input comparison-sort cutoffs, bounded reusable scratch storage and avoiding an unnecessary final array copy. Preserve ownership and provide an in-place fallback if scratch allocation fails. Deliver the best end-to-end measured variant, including its memory cost and crossover evidence.",
      "B": "Research a separate, up-to-four-worker radix backend using only threading facilities supported by the unchanged build. Use contiguous input chunks, private padded histograms and per-worker bucket offsets formed from global bucket prefixes plus preceding workers' counts. Scatter stably into disjoint ranges without per-element atomics. Recompute chunk-local histograms after each rearrangement; initial per-chunk counts are not reusable for later passes. Keep workers persistent across passes when supported, synchronize buffer swaps, and retain a serial path below a measured workload threshold. Compare bandwidth, synchronization and startup costs against the serial backend. If threading is unavailable under the fixed build, report that limitation rather than changing compiler settings.",
      "C": "Research exact case-generation acceleration independently of sorting. Preserve the two MWC recurrences, seed reset, unsigned combination, abs/conversion behavior, floating-point drand expression and resulting native integer bytes. Do not assume multiplication followed by division and integer truncation can simply be removed. Investigate independent generation blocks using mathematically derived MWC jump-ahead: each recurrence is multiplication by a modulo a*65536-1 for its applicable states, with exceptional states handled explicitly. Start each block at its exact preceding state, generate every element, and restore the exact final global states if the original quicksort remains reachable. Compare interleaved independent lanes with supported parallel blocks, including jump-ahead overhead. Require a correctness argument for the entire sequence, not merely matching sampled checksums.",
      "G": "Keep the validated baseline and best standalone candidate available. Treat A and B as alternative sorting backends; integrate the winner with C in separately measured variants rather than assuming cumulative gains. Consider generation/histogram fusion or shared worker infrastructure only after simpler combinations are correct and faster; preserve the distinction between permutation-invariant global histograms and pass-dependent chunk histograms. Use the unchanged harness and budgets, comparing complete-process timings because the baseline is only about 70 ms. Preserve stdin handling, error behavior, validation, checksum macro behavior, native-byte MD5 order and allocation fallbacks. Reason through small sizes, duplicates, signed values, uneven chunks and ownership boundaries. Never replace generation or hashing with cached results, specialize to tested sizes, change the environment, or select a slower integration merely because its components improved separately.",
      "analysis": "The relevant task is Problem B. The source reads N from stdin, generates a fixed-seed sequence, sorts it, checks ordering and conditionally computes MD5 over the sorted native-int bytes. The only measurement is a correct baseline: four correctness cases and mean runtime 0.0696778 s, with samples spanning approximately 0.069592–0.069793 s. There is no stage profile or supplied input-size distribution. Branch-heavy recursive Lomuto quicksort is therefore a strong optimization hypothesis, not a measured bottleneck. On four ARM64 cores, investigate serial radix efficiency, parallel distribution sorting and exact generator acceleration separately. Generation, allocation, validation and sequential MD5 may limit gains once sorting becomes faster."
    },
